        168,
        513,
        900,
        548
      ],
      "margin_number": 1,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Look for the subject and the two aorist participles before you go on to the main verb.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "passage_line",
      "order": 6,
      "bbox": [
        168,
        551,
        891,
        602
      ],
      "margin_number": 4,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "ἀπιόντα  Remember the difference between ὤν (‘being’) and ἰών (‘going’). The ‘going’ verb has an iota, the ‘being’ verb does not.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "passage_line",
      "order": 7,
      "bbox": [
        153,
        613,
        908,
        648
      ],
      "margin_number": 3,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "καθορᾷ ..., κατιδών  If you remove the prefix, καθ-, κατ-, you should be able to recognise the verb. ἰδ is the stem of the second aorist εἶδον.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "passage_line",
      "order": 8,
      "bbox": [
        168,
        654,
        898,
        683
      ],
      "margin_number": 8,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "λαμβάνεται τοῦ ἱματίου  He takes hold of his tunic.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 9,
      "bbox": [
        191,
        654,
        898,
        709
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "The genitive is used because he takes hold of part of his tunic (not the whole thing). This is called a partitive genitive.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 3,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
